        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "mode_agent",
      "provider": "agent_rocketride",
      "config": {
        "agent_description": "Mode Agent. Owns the final transport-mode decision for a restock leg from the LA Central warehouse to a regional hub. Delegates stockout risk analysis to risk_agent, reads the transport options from the FalkorDB supply graph, applies the cost-vs-urgency rule, and writes the decision back into the graph.",
        "instructions": [
          "You are the Mode Agent in a two-agent supply-chain negotiation. The incoming question carries a live demand-signal event as JSON context with the fields: event, hub, product, demand_multiplier. Read hub, product and demand_multiplier from that context. Never invent them.",
          "STEP 1 - DELEGATE RISK. Call the risk_agent tool (run_agent) exactly once. Pass it a single JSON object: {\"hub\": <hub>, \"product\": <product>, \"demand_multiplier\": <demand_multiplier>}. It returns JSON with: current_stock, avg_weekly_demand, effective_weekly_demand, days_of_stock_left, rail_transit_days, risk_level, stockout_gap_days. Use its numbers as-is; do not recompute them.",
          "STEP 2 - READ THE TRANSPORT OPTIONS. Use the falkordb_read tool to run this read-only Cypher, substituting the real hub name for <HUB> (single quotes, exact spelling, e.g. 'Ohio', 'Texas', 'NewJersey'): MATCH (h:Hub {name: '<HUB>'})-[:HAS_MODE]->(m:TransportMode) RETURN m.mode AS mode, m.cost AS cost, m.transit_hrs AS transit_hrs ORDER BY m.cost ASC",
          "STEP 3 - APPLY THE SELECTION RULE. This rule is fixed; do not improvise around it. If risk_level is 'low', choose 'rail' (the cheapest mode, which is the standing plan). If risk_level is 'high', consider only modes whose transit_hrs / 24 is less than or equal to days_of_stock_left, and among those choose the one with the lowest cost. If no mode qualifies, choose the mode with the lowest transit_hrs. Ties on cost break toward the lower transit_hrs.",
          "STEP 4 - WRITE THE DECISION BACK. Use the falkordb_write tool to run this Cypher, substituting <HUB>, <PRODUCT>, <MODE> and <RATIONALE> (escape any single quote inside the rationale as two single quotes): MATCH (h:Hub {name: '<HUB>'})-[:HAS_MODE]->(m:TransportMode {mode: '<MODE>'}) CREATE (s:ShipmentDecision {hub: '<HUB>', product: '<PRODUCT>', mode: '<MODE>', cost: m.cost, transit_hrs: m.transit_hrs, rationale: '<RATIONALE>', timestamp: timestamp()}) CREATE (h)-[:HAD_DECISION]->(s) RETURN s.hub AS hub, s.mode AS mode, s.cost AS cost, s.transit_hrs AS transit_hrs, s.rationale AS rationale, s.timestamp AS timestamp",
          "STEP 5 - ANSWER. Reply with a single raw JSON object and nothing else: no prose, no markdown, no code fences. Shape exactly: {\"hub\": string, \"product\": string, \"risk_level\": \"low\"|\"high\", \"stockout_gap_days\": number, \"recommended_mode\": \"rail\"|\"truck\"|\"air\", \"cost\": number, \"transit_hrs\": number, \"rationale\": string, \"written_back\": boolean}. cost and transit_hrs must be the graph's values for the chosen mode. stockout_gap_days comes from the Risk Agent. rationale is one sentence naming the tradeoff, e.g. 'Rail arrives too late to cover the outbreak-driven demand spike; truck closes the 2-day gap at a moderate cost premium over rail.' written_back is true only if STEP 4 succeeded."
        ],
        "max_waves": 12,
        "parameters": {}
      },
      "input": [
        {
          "lane": "questions",
          "from": "chat_1"
        }
      ],
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 360,
          "y": 240
        },
        "measured": {
          "width": 180,
          "height": 110
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
